> All this adds up to something I quite approve of; a good 'starter' class
> for people that isn't terribly complicated (spells are likely a lost
> cause for this guy without a ton of work) and has a lot of durability.
> He's in good shape right now -- perhaps he is the wrong target?
>
> > It sits squarely in the middle of the original
> > undifferentiated combat wombat class group and is little more than a
> > halfway point between the valkyrie and the caveman (and while those
> > two are more different from each other than they are in vanilla,
> > they're still pretty similar).
>
> Does the Barbarian need changing here, or do the Valk and Caveman?
> (the Caveman does, actually, have quite a few changes; the Valk is
> probably the visible problem here, being not much more than a slightly
> flimsier Knight with cold resistance.)


I agree that he seems the easiest, that's part of why I chose him
(though the Valk is about as easy). I do entirely agree though that
the same goal would be achieved  just as well by adjusting the Valk
and the Caveman. I haven't ascended a Spork caveman, so I can't speak
entirely to the changes, but it seems that ignoring the illiteracy
bonus and the cannibalism bonus (ideas that I quite like, but don't
seem likely to have a huge effect on normal play), the caveman is
really just a Barbarian with crappier starting inventory and a
crappier first gift.

As for the knight, I've always felt that the starting horse and saddle
and the code of conduct actually goes quite a surprisingly long way to
successfully differentiating the knight.


> While startscumming isn't productive in and of itself (if you can't
> ascend without startscumming, you likely can't ascend _with_
> startscumming), I still don't want to encourage it.

Hmmm... that's too bad. As you say, startscumming is really a non-
starter (unless you're already a skilled player and are startscumming
for the purpose of a speed run or something) and I've always thought
of it as one of the phases that a developing nethack player has to go
through (like fountain quaffing). That being said, I do really like a
certain amount of randomness in the starting inventory. There's
something exciting about rolling an Archaeologist and turning up that
marker.


> > That's it. No food and no armor. The lack of food is an encouragement
> > to get killing things right away. More on the armor issue follows.
>
> Does any role need encouragement to start doing that?  After all,
> everything in the dungeon is out to get you, for the most part, and even
> the weaker melee classes are delighted to let Fido mop up for them.

This is certainly true, though I definitely feel more comfortable
dallying at an early shop or altar if i'm playing a role that starts
with a well packed lunch. Still, starving to death is another one of
those developing player cycles. When you first start playing, food
seems impossible to come by, but one you get the hang of it, it's
mostly inconsequential unless you're playing a conduct game.


> No role in Spork will ever gain intrinsic magic resistance; in fact, I'd
> like to see it become less common on the Quest Artifacts themselves, and
> possibly more common in some other overloaded slots.  But the intrinsic
> leads down a path I will not travel.

Fair enough. I presume you did notice the caveat that these intrinsics
are only available when wearing two or fewer pieces of armour, yes? In
an "overloaded slots" sense, this is about as overloaded as they come,
with the MR essentially taking up five armour slots. Still, it does
mean that the role would have a reliable source of MR by level twelve,
and I could see how you might want to avoid that.

> ...which means that people will simply put on the ascension kit and have
> done with it, because AC -16 isn't enough in Spork.  Weight isn't an issue
> past the midgame.

Well, the example left out divine protection. With 10 points of
protection, you'd have -26 AC which is passable (or at least was the
last time I ascended in Spork). And of course, I'm certain that the
numbers I threw out would need tweaking to find the sweet spot where
it was tempting for a barb to go for the no-armour bonus without
giving them TOO much of an advantage for doing so.


> This is too fast; the Barbarian as described will not be able to handle
> the max-rate.  (I don't think you've played since I made the
> monster-clock changes, so you may wish to poke around a bit more before
> considering something like this -- specifically, you should try an
> ascension run from the Sanctum on up, to see what "max clock" really
> looks like.)

Entirely true. I really like the monster clock idea, but haven't seen
it in action, so my numbers in this regard are pure speculation.

> I like the idea of the Barbarian getting bonuses for not wearing armor,
> but there is a major problem with that: so many magical armors grant
> something useful that unless the Barbarian's bonuses make him
> significantly _better_ than a standard Ascension Kit, people simply
> aren't going to bother and are going to stick with the routine.

Well, as I said above, I feel like there SHOULD be a suite of non-
armour bonuses which would result in a barb so outfitted being only
SLIGHTLY weaker than if he were in a full ascension kit. This would
then be appealing because of weight (a small issue in the mid/endgame,
but not a total non-issue) and more importantly because of being able
to repurpose wishes that otherwise would have gone towards filling out
the AK. The obvious alternative is to give the barb penalties for
wearing armour instead of bonuses for not, but several other classes
(Monk, wizard, all spellcasters in the early game) have already gone
that route, so I was trying to think of something new.

>
> Also, my mental picture of "barbarian" includes a raggedy cloak, viking
> helmet, big furry boots, and torn gloves -- so it actually isn't necessarily
> appropriate to deny him any of those items.  I think you may have confused
> "barbarian" with "berserker" (known for rushing into battle naked).

I think of berserker as a subset of barbarian and was certainly
tending towards that particular idea while brainstorming. Particularly
because the non-berserker barbarian seems so much more similar to the
idea of the valkyrie and caveman.

> So it might be appropriate to give a barb stiff penalties for wearing
> non-leather body armor, t-shirts, or shields, and perhaps give him
> something like "berserk while drunk" as well.  To offset the decrease in
> AC and inability to benefit from DSM, barbarians could have a chance to
> evade certain types of attacks (as long as not wearing non-leather body
> armor, t-shirts, or shields) that would increase with their Dex and level.

The evading of attacks while sufficiently insufficiently armoured
sounds a lot like a more nuanced version of what I was trying to get
at with the intrinsic MC and MR and I like it a lot. Of course, I'm
excited to see whatever you do end up coming up with.

cheers.





